+\subsection{Ordinal modelling and evaluation}
+
+Ordinal labels require evaluation that respects order without hiding class-specific behavior. Proportional-odds models impose a shared-slope cumulative-link structure \cite{mccullagh1980regression}, while threshold decompositions adapt binary classifiers to ordered outcomes \cite{frank2001simple}. Ordinal assessment can combine nominal discrimination with distance-aware measures \cite{cardoso2011measuring}, including weighted kappa \cite{cohen1968weighted} and ranked probability scoring \cite{epstein1969scoring,gneiting2007strictly}. These aggregate criteria must still be accompanied by per-class results because a favorable distance-weighted score does not guarantee detection of a rare extreme category.
+
+The benchmark includes Random Forest \cite{breiman2001random}, XGBoost \cite{chen2016xgboost}, and LightGBM \cite{ke2017lightgbm} alongside linear and ordinal systems. Algorithm pedigree does not determine which metric a system will lead, so selection objectives and class-specific behavior are treated as empirical parts of the evaluation contract.
+
 \subsection{Leakage, selection, and reproducible evaluation}
 
 Leakage arises when information encodes the target or would not be available at the claimed decision point \cite{kaufman2012leakage,kapoor2023leakage}. Evaluating a configuration on the data used to select it also produces optimistic estimates, motivating nested separation of selection and evaluation \cite{cawley2010overfitting}. Reproducibility guidance calls for transparent processing, sample allocation, hyperparameters, uncertainty, and artifact reporting \cite{mitchell2019model,pineau2021improving}. Our protocol combines these principles through persisted folds, training-only selection, exact output identities, and hashes.
